{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3270 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "aee3721ff891166ad53939630da2c34ca04caf56633711f036480ad34bf8b413" :edb/db-before-t 3269 :edb/db-after-t 3270 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536582 50 #inst "2026-10-17T03:45:36.814Z" 13194139536582 17592186045436 true] [17592186101956 1064 "To multiply a matrix by a column vector, we multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product.\n\nFor example, suppose we would like to multiply \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe result of the multiplication is another column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ with the same dimensions as $\\mathbf{x}$.\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $y_1,$ the $1$st component of $\\mathbf{y}$, we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find $y_2,$ the $2$nd component of $\\mathbf{y},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139536582 17592186045436 false] [17592186101956 1064 "To multiply a matrix by a column vector, we multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product.\n\nFor example, suppose we would like to multiply the matrix $A$ by the vector $\\mathbf{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\mathbf{x}&=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\n\t\\underbrace{\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}\n\t=\n\t\\underbrace{\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}\\ast \\\\ \\ast\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{y}}\n$$\n\nThe result of this multiplication is another column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ with the same dimensions as $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $y_1$, the first component of $\\mathbf{y}$, we calculate the dot product of the first row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA\\mathbf{x} &=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}1} & {\\color{#4169E1}3} \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-3}} \\\\ \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}4}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}1}\\cdot (\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-3}})+{\\color{#4169E1}3}\\cdot \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}4}} \\\\ \\ast\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y_2$, the second component of $\\mathbf{y}$, we calculate the dot product of the second row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA\\mathbf{x} &=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}6} & {\\color{#4169E1}7}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-3}} \\\\ \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}4}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}6}\\cdot (\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-3}})+{\\color{#4169E1}7}\\cdot \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}4}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139536582 17592186045436 true] [17592186101957 1064 "An alternative, yet equivalent, procedure for multiplying a matrix by a column vector involves adding scaled columns of the matrix $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2c \\\\ 2d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a+2c \\\\ 3b+2d\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote \n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}$ \nthen we can also write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}={\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{u}_1 + {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{u}_2\n$$\n\nThe expression above is a **linear combination** of the vectors $\\mathbf{u}_1$ and $\\mathbf{u}_2,$ where the corresponding coefficients are given by the entries of the vector $\\mathbf{x}.$" 13194139536582 17592186045436 false] [17592186101957 1064 "An alternative, yet equivalent, procedure for multiplying a matrix by a column vector involves adding scaled columns of the matrix $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}3} \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\color{#4169E1}3}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+{\\color{#4169E1}2}\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2c \\\\ 2d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a+2c \\\\ 3b+2d\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad \\mathbf{u}_2=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthen we can also write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}={\\color{#4169E1}3}\\mathbf{u}_1 + {\\color{#4169E1}2} \\mathbf{u}_2\n$$\n\nThe expression above is a **linear combination** of the vectors $\\mathbf{u}_1$ and $\\mathbf{u}_2$, where the corresponding coefficients are given by the entries of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$." 13194139536582 17592186045436 true] [17592186292869 1071 "Find $Ha$ if\n$H=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$a=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatri